# Spring Boot API Endpoint Documentation Skill

Generates consistent, field-level-complete API documentation from Spring Boot source code, following a fixed
template. A mandatory self-review cross-check step runs before any documentation is finalised — this prevents
the common failure mode of missing fields, skipping validation rules, or omitting SAPI mapping rows.

---

## How to Use This Skill

### Step 1 — Gather the Source Code

The user will provide source code in one of these ways:

| Input Mode | Action |
|---|---|
| Pasted code in chat | Read directly from context |
| File(s) uploaded | `view /mnt/user-data/uploads/<file>` for each file |
| GitHub / URL | `web_fetch` the raw URL |
| Multiple files | Read ALL relevant files: Controller, Service, downstream client/SAPI, DTOs, validation classes, exception handlers |

**Always try to read:**
- `*Controller.java` — path, method, annotations, request/response types
- `*Service.java` / `*ServiceImpl.java` — business logic, downstream calls
- `*Client.java` / `*Feign*.java` / `*RestTemplate*.java` — SAPI/downstream calls
- `*Request.java`, `*Response.java`, `*DTO.java` — field definitions
- `*Validator.java`, `@Valid` / `@Validated` annotations, JSR-303 annotations
- `*ExceptionHandler.java` / `*AdviceController.java` — error mapping
- `application.yml` / `application.properties` — if referenced for config

If any file is missing and cannot be inferred, note `⚠ Source not available — inferred from context` in the relevant section.

---

### Step 2 — Parse and Extract (Internal — Do Not Output Yet)

Before writing the document, silently extract and record the following inventory. This is your working checklist.

#### 2A. Endpoint Inventory
For EACH `@GetMapping`, `@PostMapping`, `@PutMapping`, `@PatchMapping`, `@DeleteMapping`, `@RequestMapping`:
- [ ] HTTP method
- [ ] Full path (including class-level prefix)
- [ ] Summary of what it does (from Javadoc or infer from method name + logic)

#### 2B. Request Header Inventory
For each `@RequestHeader` parameter:
- [ ] Header name
- [ ] Type / format
- [ ] Required / optional (`required=false`?)
- [ ] Example value
- [ ] Description (infer from name + usage if no Javadoc)

#### 2C. Request Body Field Inventory
For EVERY field in the request DTO (recursively including nested objects):
- [ ] Field name (exact)
- [ ] Java type → API type (e.g., `String` → `string`, `LocalDate` → `string (yyyy-MM-dd)`)
- [ ] Description (from Javadoc, field name, or usage)
- [ ] Example value (realistic, not `"string"`)
- [ ] Required? (check `@NotNull`, `@NotBlank`, `@NotEmpty`, `required` flag, or nullability)

#### 2D. Validation Rule Inventory
For each field with a constraint annotation:
- [ ] Field name
- [ ] Rule (e.g., `@Size(max=50)` → "Max 50 characters")
- [ ] Error message (from annotation `message=` attribute or default)

#### 2E. Response Body Field Inventory
Same as 2C but for response DTO. Include ALL fields including nested objects.

#### 2F. Response Header Inventory
- [ ] Any `HttpServletResponse.addHeader(...)` or `ResponseEntity` headers
- [ ] If JWT is set in a header — capture the header name and how the token is built

#### 2G. JWT Inventory (if applicable)
If a JWT or token is generated/returned:
- [ ] Each claim field name
- [ ] Claim type
- [ ] Source (where the value comes from in code)
- [ ] Description

#### 2H. Error Inventory
For each exception thrown or caught:
- [ ] Exception class name
- [ ] HTTP status code it maps to
- [ ] Error response body structure
- [ ] Typical trigger condition

#### 2I. Architecture Layer Inventory
Identify:
- [ ] Controller class name
- [ ] Service class name(s)
- [ ] Repository / DAO class name(s) (if any)
- [ ] Downstream client class name(s) (SAPI)
- [ ] Config / utility classes involved

#### 2J. SAPI / Downstream Call Inventory
For EACH downstream API call:
- [ ] Target service name
- [ ] HTTP method + path
- [ ] Request headers sent
- [ ] Each request body field + where its value comes from (PAPI field, hardcoded, computed)
- [ ] Each response body field and how it maps to the PAPI response

---

### Step 3 — Self-Review Cross-Check (MANDATORY — Do Not Skip)

Before writing the document, perform this cross-check. Fix any gap found.

```
SELF-REVIEW CHECKLIST
=====================
[ ] Every field in Request DTO is in section "Request Body Structure" table — no missing rows
[ ] Every field has a realistic Example (not "string", not "N/A", not blank)
[ ] Every field has Required clearly marked Yes/No
[ ] Every @Valid / JSR-303 annotation is reflected in "Request Validation Rules"
[ ] Every field in Response DTO is in "Response Body Structure" table
[ ] Every exception handler is represented in "Error Handling" table
[ ] If JWT is present — JWT section exists with every claim documented
[ ] Every SAPI downstream call has its own sub-section under "SAPI Request"
[ ] Every SAPI request field has a "Source" column entry mapping back to PAPI or logic
[ ] DATA Mapping table covers every PAPI response field ↔ SAPI response field
[ ] Architecture table lists every identified layer
[ ] Flow diagram covers the full happy path end-to-end
```

If any checkbox would be unchecked, resolve it from the code before proceeding.
If the code is ambiguous, mark `⚠ Inferred — confirm with developer`.

---

### Step 4 — Write the Documentation

Use the template in `references/doc-template.md`.
One document per endpoint. If multiple endpoints exist, produce one document per endpoint.

**Field-level rules (enforced):**
- Table rows: never leave Description or Example blank — always write something meaningful
- Examples must be realistic values, not type placeholders
- Nested objects: expand every nested field as its own row with dot-notation name (e.g., `address.city`)
- Arrays: document the item schema, e.g., `items[].productId`
- Optional fields: mark Required = `No` and note any default value in Description

**Output format:** Markdown (`.md`).
Save to `/mnt/user-data/outputs/<EndpointName>-api-doc.md` and present to user.

---

### Step 5 — Final Output Checklist

State this explicitly at the end of your response:

```
✅ Documentation complete.
Cross-check summary:
- Request body fields documented: N
- Response body fields documented: N
- Validation rules documented: N
- Error cases documented: N
- SAPI downstream calls documented: N
- JWT section: Yes/No
- Any inferred/uncertain fields: list them or "None"
```

---

## Special Cases

### Multiple Downstream (SAPI) Calls
Create a separate `## SAPI Request — <ServiceName>` section for each call, in call order.

### Pagination
Document pagination fields (`page`, `size`, `totalElements`, etc.) as standard response fields.

### File Upload Endpoints
For `multipart/form-data`: document each form part as a row in Request Body table with type `file` or `string`.

### Async / Event-driven
If the endpoint publishes an event instead of calling SAPI directly, document the event payload under a `## Event Published` section using the same field-table format.

---

## Read the Template

Before writing any documentation, read the full template:

📄 `references/doc-template.md` — The exact markdown template to follow, section by section.
